1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
The last proof of Task 14.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
After the rollback, the health check says the right version is running.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
But a real user needs more than a health check.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:23,000
So I run the five Selenium journeys, the project's own tests, against the restored lab server on port eighty eighty eight, not against a local copy.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Setting the E2E base URL variable tells the tests where to go.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The browser is visible, and slowed down.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Journey one: a new guest registers on the restored server, and the name appears in the navigation bar.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Journey two: a wrong password is refused with the generic message, and the right password logs in.

9
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Journey three: search the rooms for two nights, book one, see it pending, then confirm it.

10
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Journey four: book, confirm, then cancel with the confirmation dialog, and see the cancelled status in My bookings.

11
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Journey five: one guest books a room, and a second guest searching the same dates sees it as booked, with no Book button.

12
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
All five journeys ran against the restored release on the lab server.

13
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Together with the health check that reports the right version, and the Ansible output, this completes the reliability workflow: provision, repeat with no changes, deploy a new release, roll back, and verify.


